+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-heading">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From seed to cup</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-heading">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p>A good cup begins long before the first roast.</p>
+
+      <div class="journal-copy" id="journal-copy">
+        <p>Our bean sourcing process begins with a simple question: where does the character of a coffee come from? Before we think about a roast profile, we look at the place where the coffee grows and the people who care for it. Altitude, soil, shade, and rainfall all shape the fruit on the tree, but so do a producer's choices throughout the season. We seek out coffees with a clear story of origin, working with growers, cooperatives, and sourcing partners who can help us understand how each lot was cultivated, harvested, and prepared.</p>
+
+        <p>Relationships matter as much as a memorable tasting note. When we consider a coffee, we ask our sourcing partners about the farm or cooperative behind it, the harvest conditions, and how the purchase supports the producer. We look for transparent conversations about pricing and the work involved in growing quality coffee. Returning to a coffee we love gives us a chance to learn across harvests, share useful feedback, and build a more dependable relationship. We also leave room for new producers and small lots that introduce us to a different expression of a familiar region.</p>
+
+        <p>Next comes the tasting table. We roast small samples and cup them side by side, paying attention to sweetness, acidity, body, and balance. A lively citrus note might make one lot a beautiful filter coffee, while a rounder coffee with cocoa-like depth could become the foundation of an espresso. We consider the processing method, too: washed coffees often offer clarity, while natural and honey processes can bring a different kind of fruit character and texture. No single method is our default. We choose the coffee whose quality and character fit the cup we want to share.</p>
+
+        <p>Care continues after selection. We plan purchases around harvest seasons and arrange for green coffee to be protected from excess moisture, heat, and long storage. Once the beans reach our roastery, we taste them again and develop a roast profile in small batches. The goal is to reveal the qualities that made us choose the lot in the first place, rather than overwhelm them. We adjust time and temperature, compare brews, and keep notes until the coffee tastes balanced both on our cupping table and in an everyday mug.</p>
+
+        <p>Sourcing is an ongoing practice, not a finished checklist. Harvests change, weather creates challenges, and a coffee's best expression can shift from one season to the next. We keep listening to our partners and tasting each arrival with fresh attention. For us, a thoughtfully sourced cup connects all of that work: the care at the farm, the decisions at the mill, the journey to our roastery, and the final brew at your table. That is the story we hope you taste in every sip.</p>
+      </div>
